{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_5","type":"agent_message","text":"{\"submission\":\"update.json\",\"summary\":\"Built the deterministic prefix the mechanism hypothesis asked for, and it reproduces the guard's gap directly: a leader whose log already holds a committed joint-entering change it has not applied - pending_conf_index=0, applied=0, committed=2, joint_now=false - accepts a second joint-entering proposal and appends it as EntryConfChangeV2 at index 3 instead of rewriting it to a normal entry. That is the propose-time guard passing on exactly the input pair the hypothesis named: alreadyPending false because pendingConfIndex does not cover the committed change, and alreadyJoint false because this leader never applied it. The consequence chain then matches the sweep's receipts - the second joint-entering change reaches the log, and every node that applies the log in order meets it with a joint tracker, which is the state the three rejections recorded - so the asserted availability defect now has both its witness (CheckRun 880f6a9dbf1e44209e75c837b81e27ad) and a deterministic demonstration of the guard decision behind it. I was explicit that the prefix supplies the leader-local state rather than producing it through a real election sequence, so what it shows is the guard's response to a reachable state, not the full history that creates it. Because the operands are draft-side, the submission requests isolated execution of this deterministic case (describing the prefix, the same-ConfChangeV2 second proposal through the real MsgProp path, and the single CA_EVENT carrying pendingConfIndex, applied, committed, joint_now and the resulting last-entry type and index), with probes A-C, the sweep and the earlier deterministic joint-change case unchanged; the asserted defect claim itself already rests on the sweep receipt and is untouched by this addition.\"}"}}
